alex: Quick question — running into ESM vs CJS import errors when bundling the Node/TS SDK for Next edge functions. Error: "SyntaxError: Unexpected token 'export'" at runtime. Anyone seen a small wrapper pattern that works across Next (server/edge) and Vite dev builds?

maya: Yep, saw this last week. Two approaches: 1) provide a tiny runtime-aware wrapper that dynamically imports the right entrypoint, or 2) publish an ESM-only browser-safe build and keep node-specific code in a separate conditional export. For immediate unblock, wrapper is easiest.

jin: Example wrapper I used (keeps at build-time tree-shakeable):
```ts
// src/lib/redwoodClient.ts
export async function getRedwoodClient() {
  // Next sets NEXT_RUNTIME to 'edge' for edge functions, otherwise 'node'
  const isEdge = (process.env.NEXT_RUNTIME === 'edge') || (typeof globalThis.WebSocket !== 'undefined' && typeof process === 'undefined');
  if (isEdge) {
    // dynamic import so Vite/Next can inline ESM-friendly bundle for edge
    const mod = await import('@redwood-inference/edge-sdk');
    return mod.createClient?.() ?? mod.default ?? mod;
  }
  // node path (CJS-friendly)
  const mod = await import('@redwood-inference/node-sdk');
  return mod.createClient?.() ?? mod.default ?? mod;
}
```

sam: Couple notes:
- Use dynamic import so bundlers don't try to evaluate incompatible syntax at build time.
- For Next 13/14 edge functions you can also gate by process.env.NEXT_RUNTIME at runtime.
- Add a tiny entry in package.json exports so that `import '@redwood-inference/edge-sdk'` resolves correctly in ESM builds.

alex: Thanks, that helps. Two followups: auth and streaming. Auth setup: we want to avoid exposing API keys to the client. For edge SSR (server-side) we need environment variable access. Any preferred env var naming or pattern?

maya: Keep it server-only env var like REDWOOD_API_KEY (do NOT prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC). For serverless/edge, Vercel provides env to edge via runtime config but watch limits on secrets size. Document the explicit note in SDK README: "Use server-only envs; do not put keys in browser-exposed vars."

jin: Streaming quirks: Edge runtime uses Web Streams. Our Node SDK currently emits Node Readable streams for streaming responses, which breaks in the Edge runtime. Workaround: detect if response.body is a ReadableStream and use getReader(). Example:
```ts
// edge streaming helper
async function streamToText(stream) {
  if (!stream) return '';
  if (typeof stream.getReader === 'function') {
    // Web ReadableStream (Edge / browser)
    const reader = stream.getReader();
    let out = '';
    while (true) {
      const { value, done } = await reader.read();
      if (done) break;
      out += new TextDecoder().decode(value);
    }
    return out;
  }
  // Node Readable stream path (server)
  const chunks = [];
  for await (const chunk of stream) chunks.push(typeof chunk === 'string' ? chunk : chunk.toString());
  return chunks.join('');
}
```

alex: Good call. One more: retry/backoff. Our default retry uses setTimeout and exponential backoff; setTimeout exists in Edge but some runtimes constrain timers. Any suggestions to make retry logic portable?

sam: Use AbortController with a small delay helper that uses Promise + setTimeout, but keep timeouts configurable and provide a no-timer fallback that does a single retry. Also expose a pluggable backoff function so platform-specific infra (like Vercel edge) can override to faster/fewer attempts.

